const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Literature Search and Citation Service","serviceType":"Search strategy design, database searching, grey literature and reference verification","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/literature-search-and-citation/","description":"Searches built in each database’s own vocabulary, tested against studies you already have, and reported with date.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Which databases should I search?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Chosen from the question rather than counted. For clinical questions about randomised evidence, MEDLINE, Embase and the Cochrane Library cover most of it, with trial registries for completed-but-unpublished trials. Scopus or Web of Science add coverage outside the biomedical core and let you chase citations forward. CINAHL matters for nursing and allied health, PsycINFO for mental health, and regional databases — LILACS, African Journals Online, CNKI, KoreaMed — matter considerably more than most English-language reviews acknowledge. What does not improve a search is adding a sixth database and running the same badly translated strategy in it. Two well-built searches retrieve more than five pasted ones. A useful number to ask for afterwards is how many records were unique to each database: that is what tells you whether the Embase subscription earned its place for your question, and for some questions it plainly does not."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why can't I just use the same search in every database?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because the controlled vocabularies are different thesauri, not dialects of one. MEDLINE, PubMed and Cochrane CENTRAL use MeSH; Embase uses Emtree, which is Elsevier’s own thesaurus with different headings and a different tree structure; Scopus and Web of Science have no controlled vocabulary at all. A MeSH term pasted into Embase will usually map to nothing, and the crucial detail is that it fails silently — the search still runs, still returns records, and the count still looks plausible. It is simply retrieving a different set. Field tags differ too, as does limit syntax and the behaviour of truncation and adjacency operators. The only reliable protection is to rebuild the strategy in each database and then test each version against papers you already know belong in the results. In the sample above, two of four known papers were missed on the first attempt for two different reasons, and neither would have been visible from the result count."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I know if my search is any good?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Two checks, one free and one cheap. The free one is the known-item test: pick three or four papers that definitely belong in your results and check that the search retrieves all of them in every database. If it misses one, the strategy is wrong rather than the field being thin, and you have found that out in ten minutes. The cheap one is peer review of the strategy against the PRESS checklist, which is the published instrument for exactly this and which catches the things a known-item test does not — a truncation that has quietly broadened a concept, a Boolean operator in the wrong place, a filter losing records, a line that should be OR and is AND. Both are worth doing before the search is run rather than after four thousand records have been screened. A third signal, after the fact: if citation chasing turns up several studies your database search missed, the strategy had a gap."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Should I limit to English?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Only with a reason, and always with a declaration. Restricting to English excludes trials that exist, and it does so unevenly — by topic, by region and by intervention, so the exclusion is not random noise. It is sometimes defensible on resource grounds, particularly for a rapid review with a stated deadline; it is never defensible silently. If you apply it, say so in the methods, repeat it in the limitations, and say what you think it cost. There is a cheaper middle route that most people do not consider: do not limit the search, screen the non-English titles and abstracts with whatever translation is available, and then decide. You frequently find that the non-English studies are few enough to translate properly, and a review that includes them is materially stronger. Where translation is needed, we can arrange appraisal of non-English studies as an addition."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between formatting a reference and verifying it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Formatting checks that the citation is correctly written in the target style. Verification opens the cited work and reads it against the statement it is attached to. They are different jobs, they cost differently, and only the second one catches the things that matter. In the sample audit above, sixty-two references produced nine formatting errors — corrected silently, nobody cares — and seven substantive problems: four claims the cited paper does not support, two citations pointing at the wrong study entirely, and one retracted paper. The four unsupported claims are the ones worth dwelling on, because in every case the claim had been inherited from another paper’s citation rather than read at first hand, and one traced back through three papers to a source that says the opposite. No author in that chain was careless. It is simply what happens when everybody cites the paper before rather than the study itself, and reading the source is the only thing that stops it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What tools do you use for reference management?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"EndNote, Zotero, Mendeley and RefWorks, and we deliver RIS alongside whichever you use so the library survives you changing your mind. Two practical points matter more than the choice. First, keep one library per project rather than one per author — merging three libraries at submission is where duplicate and wrong-year citations come from, and it always happens under time pressure. Second, a reference manager reproduces whatever is in the record it imported, including the record’s errors: a wrong year in a database record becomes a wrong year in your bibliography, and the software has no way of knowing. That is why verification is against the source rather than against the record. Where a project has inherited three libraries in different formats, migration and deduplication is a service we sell on its own, and it is usually a better first purchase than anything else."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I perform a systematic literature search step by step?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Break the question into two or three concepts — rarely more, because each concept combined with AND is a filter and four filters produce twelve records. For each concept, gather free-text synonyms from the literature rather than from a thesaurus: read the titles and abstracts of papers you already have and harvest the words those authors used, including older terminology and regional variants. Then pick the controlled terms from each database’s own vocabulary. Combine synonyms within a concept using OR, and concepts using AND. Set truncation per term rather than globally. Build the strategy separately in each database, then test each against three or four known papers and fix what it misses. Run it, save every strategy with its date, interface and line-by-line counts, deduplicate and report the rate. Then chase citations forward and backward, and search registries and grey literature if the question needs them. The order matters: everything before “run it” is where the quality is decided."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you re-run a search I did two years ago?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If it was saved properly, in an afternoon. If it was not, it has to be rebuilt from whatever you have — and the numbers will not match the original, which is exactly the problem you were trying to avoid. This is why a search log carries the date, the interface and the count at every line: a strategy run through PubMed and the same strategy run through Ovid MEDLINE return different numbers, and so does the same strategy through the same interface a year later. Re-running matters more often than people expect. A clinical evaluation under the EU MDR is updated rather than finished, so the search recurs on a cycle. A systematic review submitted eighteen months ago will be asked for a search refresh at revision. A guideline is reviewed on a schedule. Where we built the original, a refresh is quoted on the saved strategy and is a fraction of the first search. Where somebody else built it, the first job is usually working out what they actually ran."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Physician writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Literature search and citation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/literature-search-and-citation/"}]}];

